WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.840
Thank you, Tim.

00:30.000 --> 00:35.000
Tell me, Mr. Dill, how did you happen to discover this amazing talent of yours?

00:35.000 --> 00:40.240
Well, I'll tell you, Mr. Higerman, in the year of 1914, in the month of February, I

00:40.240 --> 00:44.840
was coming home from school and my hands got very cold, and you're seeing I had no mittens.

00:44.840 --> 00:48.160
And I began, of course, pressing them together to get them warm, and that wouldn't do.

00:48.160 --> 00:51.120
Well, pretty soon I began squeezing them, and I was surprised to think that I could

00:51.120 --> 00:52.480
make a few different sounds.

00:52.480 --> 00:56.960
Well, then I began playing every day, and pretty soon I could play Yankee Doodle clear

00:56.960 --> 00:57.960
through.

00:57.960 --> 01:01.840
That was the only tune I could play for three whole years, and the next tune I could play

01:01.840 --> 01:06.240
was Marching Through Georgia, then The Tale of the Lonesome Pine.

01:06.240 --> 01:10.680
Then for about, everybody wondered then how I could do it, and it would bother me so much

01:10.680 --> 01:15.760
that for about six years I put it away, until I met an old showman that come up to Travers

01:15.760 --> 01:19.120
once, and he says, can you do your old novelty any more, Cecil?

01:19.120 --> 01:23.840
I said, I don't know, but I'll try, and I tried, of course, and it come right natural

01:23.840 --> 01:26.040
to me after letting it go for six years.

01:26.040 --> 01:29.720
He said, well, if I was you, he said, I would practice it up and see what I could do some

01:29.720 --> 01:56.360
time in the States when I was in Chicago.

